  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 301 PM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nA transitory, low amplitude ridge overnight Saturday into \nSunday, will bring a brief period of mainly dry conditions to \nthe area. However, gusty southerly winds east of a deepening \nsurface trough will continue to push low level moisture into the\nforecast area from the south leading to mostly cloudy \nconditions across the area on Sunday. This expected cloud cover \nwill lead to highs only reaching around 60 for most areas. Some \nmid 60s may be achieved in the northwest, INVOF the surface \ntrough where some limited clearing is possible by Sunday \nafternoon. The latest GFS and NAM12 solns have PWATS approaching\n> than 0.90 inches Sunday afternoon. This is well above the max\npercentile for KLBF November 1st per sounding climatology. \nShowers and even embedded thunderstorms will increase in \ncoverage Sunday night into Monday as a lead disturbance lifts \ninto central and eastern Nebraska. The latest NBM ensemble \nforecast has a 90+% chance of reaching at least a quarter inch \nof 24 hr QPF ending at 6 PM CT Monday for locations along and \neast of highway 83. That being said, 90+ pops are on track for \nthese areas. Further west to the panhandle, there is a 90+% \nchance of reaching 0.10\" of QPF during the same time frame. \nInitialized likely pops for these areas looks good based on the \ndeterministic and NBM ensembles. One final note, the latest \nECMWF ensemble has EFI of 0.8 to 0.9 and and SOT of 1-2 over \nnortheastern portions of the forecast area Monday night into \nTuesday, so will have to continue to monitor the northeast for \nheavy rain potential. Finally, gusty northerly winds appear \nprobable Monday and Tuesday. Both days have a better than 80% \nchance of exceeding wind gusts over 30 MPH per the latest NBM \nensemble forecast. Locations over the central and western \nSandhills have a 50- 70% chance of exceeding wind gusts of 50 \nMPH Monday and Tuesday. Will highlight the wind threat in the \nlatest HWO. After Tuesday, there is a continued threat for \nshowers, mainly over eastern portions of the forecast area. \nHighs per the WPC initialization are in the lower 50s Wednesday \nand Thursday. This is near the 25%ile of the NBM forecast. Based\non the deterministic 12 z GFS and 00z EC solns, the WPC \nforecast leans heavily on the EC soln late next week.",
